WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.767
Welcome to Module 8 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:08.525
convert Kerberos accounts to group and
delegated managed service accounts.

3
00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:11.557
This module is the
on-premises part of Gate 5,

4
00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:15.262
Conversion, with Chapter 6
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:19.671
You'll read each Kerberos
account's reviewed disposition,

6
00:00:19.671 --> 00:00:24.080
the owner's decision from Module
3, plan its move to a G M S A or,

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.927
on a Windows Server
2025 domain, a D M S A,

8
00:00:26.927 --> 00:00:32.622
list what stays on the Kerberos island with
an end date and, where it has S P N's,

9
00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:38.500
an S P N owner, and reconcile every managed
account with the program's end state.

10
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.400
Then you'll see the write path run
a plan only by its approved hash.

11
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.300
The domain is fictional, and the
apply changes only a fake, in memory.

12
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.450
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.963
Some workloads can't leave Kerberos yet.

14
00:00:50.963 --> 00:00:53.905
A traditional service
account with an S P N and a

15
00:00:53.905 --> 00:00:57.150
password that never expires
is the riskiest of them.

16
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:02.135
Microsoft Learn's guidance on securing
group managed service accounts says

17
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:06.575
a G M S A gives the same workload a
password that Windows changes for it.

18
00:01:06.575 --> 00:01:12.083
And Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts says a D M S A, on a

19
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:18.125
Windows Server 2025 domain, supersedes
the old account and disables its password.

20
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:22.435
But a G M S A works only while
Active Directory is authoritative,

21
00:01:22.435 --> 00:01:26.662
which is why the program's guidance
gives every one an end date.

22
00:01:26.662 --> 00:01:28.588
So converting isn't the end.

23
00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:34.496
Every G M S A and D M S A must either
move to a workload identity, retire,

24
00:01:34.496 --> 00:01:37.827
or sit on a dated Kerberos island list,

25
00:01:37.827 --> 00:01:42.124
with an end date and, where it
has S P N's, an S P N owner.

26
00:01:42.124 --> 00:01:44.215
In this module you plan all three,

27
00:01:44.215 --> 00:01:48.075
and you see the write path
refuse a plan nobody approved.

28
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.578
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

29
00:01:52.578 --> 00:01:54.965
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

30
00:01:54.965 --> 00:01:58.490
You'll name the four dispositions
a Kerberos account can have,

31
00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.803
and what each one needs.

32
00:01:59.803 --> 00:02:04.243
You'll explain why a D M S A can't be
used for a managed service account,

33
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:07.553
or for an account with
unconstrained delegation.

34
00:02:07.553 --> 00:02:12.553
You'll read the plan, the island list and
the reconciliation for a fictional domain.

35
00:02:12.553 --> 00:02:17.465
And you'll show that the write path runs
only a plan whose hash was approved, sends

36
00:02:17.465 --> 00:02:22.153
each write only as the pipeline or the
person it belongs to, and never deletes.

37
00:02:22.153 --> 00:02:28.699
Before you start, finish Module 1, because
the lab reads Chapter 1's register, and

38
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:34.742
Module 3, where the matrix sends Kerberos-only
accounts to a G M S A or a D M S A.

39
00:02:34.742 --> 00:02:39.577
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and a clone.

40
00:02:39.577 --> 00:02:40.870
No tenant.

41
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.445
Unit 1 is the guide's gate.

42
00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:51.863
Its Step 5 is the program's guidance:
build the target, give it two human owners,

43
00:02:51.863 --> 00:02:57.195
run old and new in parallel for thirty
days, then disable the legacy account.

44
00:02:57.195 --> 00:02:58.295
Never delete it.

45
00:02:58.295 --> 00:03:02.233
Step 2 adds the
prerequisites for a G M S A.

46
00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:05.489
The forest needs one K D
S root key, created once:

47
00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:09.687
Microsoft Learn calls it a
one-time operation in the forest,

48
00:03:09.687 --> 00:03:14.056
and its page on creating the
key says domain controllers wait

49
00:03:14.056 --> 00:03:18.083
up to ten hours after it's created
before they allow a G M S A.

50
00:03:18.083 --> 00:03:23.521
And a G M S A's name must be unique
in the forest, not just the domain.

51
00:03:23.521 --> 00:03:27.688
Step 3's seventh step covers the
Active Directory side of credentials:

52
00:03:27.688 --> 00:03:32.862
Learn's guidance on securing group managed
service accounts says Windows changes the

53
00:03:32.862 --> 00:03:35.161
password every thirty days by default,

54
00:03:35.161 --> 00:03:38.682
and the interval can be set only
when the account is created.

55
00:03:38.682 --> 00:03:41.605
The rollback for Step 5 is
to re-enable the legacy

56
00:03:41.605 --> 00:03:44.820
account and revert the
application's configuration.

57
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.675
A delegated account runs
the same thirty days:

58
00:03:47.675 --> 00:03:52.710
Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts says to wait at least two

59
00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.640
ticket lifetimes, equivalent
to fourteen days,

60
00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.045
and recommends four, twenty-eight days.

61
00:03:58.045 --> 00:04:00.805
The program's thirty
days is its own choice,

62
00:04:00.805 --> 00:04:04.790
longer than that, and it
completes a migration only then.

63
00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.740
Unit 2 starts with the
context the code depends on.

64
00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.315
Every Kerberos account has
one of four dispositions,

65
00:04:15.315 --> 00:04:19.621
and it's the application owner's
decision from Module 3, D N P three,

66
00:04:19.621 --> 00:04:24.252
which this chapter reads from Chapter
3's run, never from a second list.

67
00:04:24.252 --> 00:04:30.023
Replatform: to a G M S A or a D M S A,
converted here, with the new account's name,

68
00:04:30.023 --> 00:04:34.228
the workload and its hosts
in one more reviewed file;

69
00:04:34.228 --> 00:04:39.314
to a managed identity or an app
registration, Chapter 5 moves it.

70
00:04:39.314 --> 00:04:44.774
Keep: it stays, with an end date no later
than G 5, or the island's end of life

71
00:04:44.774 --> 00:04:50.327
where the program keeps a Kerberos island,
and an S P N owner if it has S P N's.

72
00:04:50.327 --> 00:04:55.989
Keep Kerberos: it stays on Kerberos with
the authorizing official's acceptance, an

73
00:04:55.989 --> 00:05:01.152
S P N owner, and an end date with the same
bound, recorded by decision D oh eight.

74
00:05:01.152 --> 00:05:05.015
Or retire, through the plan's
reviewed decommission process.

75
00:05:05.015 --> 00:05:09.877
The program adds rules of its own,
its design guidance, not Microsoft's.

76
00:05:09.877 --> 00:05:12.303
The disposition is Chapter 3's.

77
00:05:12.303 --> 00:05:16.967
Who may retrieve a managed password
is reviewed data: for a G M S A,

78
00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:21.452
one domain local group of its
hosts, never a computer account.

79
00:05:21.452 --> 00:05:24.828
Every new account follows the
managed service account standard.

80
00:05:24.828 --> 00:05:29.016
Every write carries its SailPoint
request and ServiceNow change.

81
00:05:29.016 --> 00:05:35.427
The pipeline's own G M S A sends what it
can, and a person sends the D M S A steps.

82
00:05:35.427 --> 00:05:39.941
A D M S A migration runs thirty
days before it completes.

83
00:05:39.941 --> 00:05:43.015
Every account that stays
ends within its bound.

84
00:05:43.015 --> 00:05:47.263
And never delete: the old account is
disabled and moved to a holding O U,

85
00:05:47.263 --> 00:05:51.130
and a person deletes it through
the decommission ticket.

86
00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.593
The check runs ten rules,
N P I K R B oh one to ten.

87
00:05:59.593 --> 00:06:04.118
Oh one, oh four and oh eight come from
Microsoft Learn's pages on the root key,

88
00:06:04.118 --> 00:06:09.236
delegated managed service accounts and
securing group managed service accounts;

89
00:06:09.236 --> 00:06:11.980
the others are the
program's design guidance.

90
00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.116
Oh one: there's no K D S root key,

91
00:06:14.116 --> 00:06:17.830
or one that became usable
less than ten hours ago.

92
00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:21.204
Oh two: a Kerberos
account has no disposition

93
00:06:21.204 --> 00:06:24.305
in Chapter 3's decisions,
or an unknown one.

94
00:06:24.305 --> 00:06:29.988
Oh three: the accounts allowed to retrieve
a managed password in Active Directory

95
00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:34.918
differ from the reviewed list, or a G M
S A's list isn't one group of its hosts.

96
00:06:34.918 --> 00:06:38.592
Oh four is the one to
remember: an unusable target.

97
00:06:38.592 --> 00:06:43.425
Learn's overview and F A Q for delegated
managed service accounts say a D M S A

98
00:06:43.425 --> 00:06:46.075
can't come from a managed service account,

99
00:06:46.075 --> 00:06:51.218
can't serve an account with unconstrained
delegation, which stops working once

100
00:06:51.218 --> 00:06:56.518
the migration completes, and needs Windows
Server 2025 on the hosts and the domain.

101
00:06:56.518 --> 00:07:02.444
Oh five and oh six: an account that stays
with no S P N owner where Chapter 3 asks

102
00:07:02.444 --> 00:07:07.429
for one, or no end date, or one past
its bound: G 5, or the island's end of

103
00:07:07.429 --> 00:07:13.355
life where the program keeps a Kerberos
island, for every account that stays.

104
00:07:13.355 --> 00:07:16.955
Oh seven: an account not
reconciled with the end state.

105
00:07:16.955 --> 00:07:21.942
Oh eight warns about a privileged group,
and oh nine hands SQL Server accounts on.

106
00:07:21.942 --> 00:07:25.707
And ten: a write with no
SailPoint request or ServiceNow

107
00:07:25.707 --> 00:07:29.910
change isn't planned at all;
the plan records it as blocked.

108
00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.025
Each account becomes one
action in the plan, or,

109
00:07:37.025 --> 00:07:41.010
for a superseded account, a
disable and a move together.

110
00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:46.120
The G M S A path: create it in the service
account O U with a thirty-day interval,

111
00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.735
once the root key is ready and its
retrievers are one reviewed group.

112
00:07:50.735 --> 00:07:54.998
Wait while the server owner installs
it and changes the service's identity.

113
00:07:54.998 --> 00:07:58.398
Then a thirty-day parallel
run, the program's guidance,

114
00:07:58.398 --> 00:08:02.572
and only then disable the old account
and move it to the holding O U.

115
00:08:02.572 --> 00:08:06.134
The pipeline's own G M
S A sends those writes.

116
00:08:06.134 --> 00:08:12.060
The D M S A path: create it, if it passes
every limit, and start the migration.

117
00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.110
After the thirty-day
parallel run, complete it.

118
00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:20.047
Microsoft Learn's overview of
delegated managed service accounts says

119
00:08:20.047 --> 00:08:24.573
completing disables the old account
and moves its S P N's to the D M S A.

120
00:08:24.573 --> 00:08:26.472
A person sends those steps.

121
00:08:26.472 --> 00:08:30.297
Set retrievers fixes a list that
differs from the reviewed one.

122
00:08:30.297 --> 00:08:33.948
Anything else is recorded with
its reason, and writes nothing.

123
00:08:33.948 --> 00:08:39.240
The same inputs always give the same
bytes, so a reviewer approves one hash.

124
00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.415
These are the kit's design.

125
00:08:44.415 --> 00:08:47.027
Two outputs carry the chapter's end date.

126
00:08:47.027 --> 00:08:50.826
The island list holds every
account that stays on Kerberos,

127
00:08:50.826 --> 00:08:54.005
with its disposition from
Chapter 3, its S P N owner,

128
00:08:54.005 --> 00:08:56.485
its end date and the date that bounds it,

129
00:08:56.485 --> 00:09:01.059
the authorizing official's acceptance
for an account kept on Kerberos,

130
00:09:01.059 --> 00:09:05.865
and whether it's ready for P four point
six, the program's S P N retirement work.

131
00:09:05.865 --> 00:09:08.852
The hand-off is ready
only when every row is.

132
00:09:08.852 --> 00:09:13.035
The reconciliation covers
every G M S A and D M S A,

133
00:09:13.035 --> 00:09:17.003
existing or planned, and
says where it ends up.

134
00:09:17.003 --> 00:09:20.081
Writing to the domain
lives in a separate script,

135
00:09:20.081 --> 00:09:24.008
and it refuses to run unless the
plan's hash is the approved one,

136
00:09:24.008 --> 00:09:27.626
the plan is for this domain and
no more than seven days old,

137
00:09:27.626 --> 00:09:32.014
and each write carries its SailPoint
request and ServiceNow change.

138
00:09:32.014 --> 00:09:34.003
Each write also says who sends it.

139
00:09:34.003 --> 00:09:40.268
Run as the pipeline, which must be the
pipeline's own G M S A, it sends the G M S

140
00:09:40.268 --> 00:09:47.064
A creations, retriever changes, disables
and moves, and refuses the D M S A steps.

141
00:09:47.064 --> 00:09:52.377
Run as a person, it sends the D M S
A steps and refuses the pipeline's.

142
00:09:52.377 --> 00:09:55.353
So one approved plan is
applied in two runs.

143
00:09:55.353 --> 00:09:59.452
Nothing that removes, undoes
or resets is ever run.

144
00:09:59.452 --> 00:10:02.262
And the read holds no
domain administration:

145
00:10:02.262 --> 00:10:07.500
Microsoft Learn's page for the root key
class says its default security descriptor

146
00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:11.068
grants only Enterprise Admins,
Domain Admins and SYSTEM,

147
00:10:11.068 --> 00:10:16.430
so the read gets a delegated read of the
key's start and create times, nothing more.

148
00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:20.905
Try it.

149
00:10:20.905 --> 00:10:26.580
Lab 1 runs in the non-person identity
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

150
00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:31.837
Its step one makes the three runs Chapter
6 reads: Chapter 1's register, Chapter

151
00:10:31.837 --> 00:10:36.930
2's owners and Chapter 3's decisions, so
the chapter's steps are one ahead here.

152
00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:38.080
It prints nothing.

153
00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:42.501
The three folders hold the register,
the owners and the owners' D

154
00:10:42.501 --> 00:10:47.092
N P three decisions, which Chapter
6 reads instead of a second list.

155
00:10:47.092 --> 00:10:50.667
Step two runs Microsoft
Learn's published gMSA,

156
00:10:50.667 --> 00:10:56.442
dMSA and root key procedures against the
stand-ins: seventeen pass, none fail.

157
00:10:56.442 --> 00:10:59.080
Step three lists what each task runs.

158
00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:04.246
The read task, kerberos island, runs
only the directory Get cmdlets:

159
00:11:04.246 --> 00:11:09.505
computers, domain controllers,
objects, service accounts and users.

160
00:11:09.505 --> 00:11:11.531
The write task, kerberos apply,

161
00:11:11.531 --> 00:11:16.556
adds the cmdlets that create and set a
service account, start and complete a

162
00:11:16.556 --> 00:11:21.581
migration, disable an account, and move
a disabled account to the holding O U.

163
00:11:21.581 --> 00:11:26.930
Notice what's missing from both lists: no
cmdlet that removes or undoes anything.

164
00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:30.530
The output on the slide is
trimmed and wrapped to fit.

165
00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:37.905
Step four checks the Kerberos
accounts and builds the plan.

166
00:11:37.905 --> 00:11:41.305
Nine accounts are in scope,
and the root key is ready.

167
00:11:41.305 --> 00:11:45.267
There are six findings, one
warning and two information items.

168
00:11:45.267 --> 00:11:48.682
The plan has ten actions,
five of them writes:

169
00:11:48.682 --> 00:11:53.804
one completed migration, two new G
M S A's, and two retriever changes.

170
00:11:53.804 --> 00:11:54.980
Nothing is sent.

171
00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:59.830
Run by counts the writes by who sends
them: four the pipeline's, one a person's.

172
00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:02.130
The island list holds six accounts,

173
00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:06.255
four of them ready for P four point
six, so the hand-off isn't ready.

174
00:12:06.255 --> 00:12:09.893
The reconciliation leaves
one account unreconciled.

175
00:12:09.893 --> 00:12:12.483
The dispositions come
from Chapter 3's run,

176
00:12:12.483 --> 00:12:16.331
and the program dates file must
have the hash that run recorded.

177
00:12:16.331 --> 00:12:20.205
The plan's hash is the same on
every run with these inputs.

178
00:12:20.205 --> 00:12:22.530
Step five reads the findings.

179
00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:26.665
svc webfront is trusted for
unconstrained delegation,

180
00:12:26.665 --> 00:12:30.620
which stops working once a
D M S A migration completes,

181
00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:36.283
so in Chapter 3 its owner kept it on
Kerberos, with the authorizing official's

182
00:12:36.283 --> 00:12:40.867
acceptance; N P I K R B oh five flags
it because it has no S P N owner.

183
00:12:40.867 --> 00:12:47.368
Oh five and oh six flag svc intranet:
Chapter 3 re-platforms it to a G M S A,

184
00:12:47.368 --> 00:12:51.930
but it has no S P N owner and
its end date is after G 5.

185
00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:56.595
The kept G M S A's end in August
twenty twenty-seven, after D oh eight,

186
00:12:56.595 --> 00:13:02.317
which bounds no end date, and before the
island's end of life, which is their bound.

187
00:13:02.317 --> 00:13:05.893
And oh eight warns that gmsa
ops is in Domain Admins.

188
00:13:05.893 --> 00:13:08.951
N P I K R B oh three flags
gmsa ops and gmsa web,

189
00:13:08.951 --> 00:13:14.633
because Active Directory still names
computer accounts as their retrievers,

190
00:13:14.633 --> 00:13:18.760
where the reviewed list
names each one's hosts group.

191
00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.222
Step six reads the plan.

192
00:13:24.222 --> 00:13:26.910
Ten actions, and these
are the five writes.

193
00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.048
Set retrievers for gmsa ops and gmsa web.

194
00:13:30.048 --> 00:13:34.738
Create a G M S A for the managed
service account msa print oh one,

195
00:13:34.738 --> 00:13:39.151
outbound-only because it holds
no S P N, and for svc intranet,

196
00:13:39.151 --> 00:13:43.473
both in the service account O
U with a thirty-day interval.

197
00:13:43.473 --> 00:13:47.170
And complete the D M S A
migration for svc h r sync,

198
00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:52.859
whose migration started forty days
ago, past the thirty-day parallel run.

199
00:13:52.859 --> 00:13:56.809
Each write carries its SailPoint
request and ServiceNow change,

200
00:13:56.809 --> 00:13:58.272
or it wouldn't be planned.

201
00:13:58.272 --> 00:14:04.422
gmsa ops, gmsa web and svc webfront stay
on the island, as Chapter 3 decided.

202
00:14:04.422 --> 00:14:08.060
The other actions are recorded with
their reasons and write nothing.

203
00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.785
Step seven reads the island
list and the reconciliation.

204
00:14:11.785 --> 00:14:15.860
Six island rows, each under the
name the account will end up with

205
00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:19.935
and with its disposition, four of
them ready for P four point six.

206
00:14:19.935 --> 00:14:24.529
gmsa intranet has no S P N owner
and ends at the end of 2028,

207
00:14:24.529 --> 00:14:29.923
so it isn't ready, and its
reconciliation outcome is unreconciled.

208
00:14:29.923 --> 00:14:35.042
svc webfront carries the authorizing
official's acceptance from Chapter 3,

209
00:14:35.042 --> 00:14:38.240
so it becomes ready once
it has an S P N owner.

210
00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:45.653
Step eight is the write path's
dry run, as the pipeline.

211
00:14:45.653 --> 00:14:49.085
You take the plan's hash, which
in a real run a second person

212
00:14:49.085 --> 00:14:52.665
would review and pass to you,
and give it to the action script.

213
00:14:52.665 --> 00:14:56.703
It reports four would-send
lines, the pipeline's writes,

214
00:14:56.703 --> 00:15:01.190
and refuses the fifth: the D M S
A's completion is a person's step.

215
00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:02.303
It sends nothing.

216
00:15:02.303 --> 00:15:05.922
Step nine applies it to the
fake, first as the pipeline,

217
00:15:05.922 --> 00:15:08.882
which sends its four
writes, then as a person,

218
00:15:08.882 --> 00:15:14.228
which sends the completed migration for
svc h r sync and refuses the other four.

219
00:15:14.228 --> 00:15:16.653
Each run re-reads every write.

220
00:15:16.653 --> 00:15:20.389
Then you pass a hash of zeros,
a hash nobody approved.

221
00:15:20.389 --> 00:15:24.139
The script refuses: the plan's
hash isn't the approved one,

222
00:15:24.139 --> 00:15:26.665
and it won't run a plan nobody reviewed.

223
00:15:26.665 --> 00:15:30.665
Pasted line by line, your shell
carries on after the refusal.

224
00:15:30.665 --> 00:15:35.803
Step ten runs every test: five
hundred forty-nine pass, none fail.

225
00:15:35.803 --> 00:15:37.990
Then delete the output folder.

226
00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:46.611
Lab 2 moves to a test Active
Directory domain, never production,

227
00:15:46.611 --> 00:15:52.326
with at least one Windows Server 2025
domain controller, a Windows Server

228
00:15:52.326 --> 00:15:57.478
2025 member server, an existing K D S
root key, and Chapter 1's Lab 2 run.

229
00:15:57.478 --> 00:16:01.842
Make a traditional test account,
a test managed service account,

230
00:16:01.842 --> 00:16:04.477
and the G M S A's domain
local hosts group.

231
00:16:04.477 --> 00:16:09.357
Write the three inputs, with a test
SailPoint request and ServiceNow change,

232
00:16:09.357 --> 00:16:11.603
and the test domain's standard file.

233
00:16:11.603 --> 00:16:16.028
Grant the read only the root key's
two date attributes, nothing else.

234
00:16:16.028 --> 00:16:19.459
Run the check, then apply the
plan as a person with Domain

235
00:16:19.459 --> 00:16:22.965
Admins or equivalent rights,
with the test domain switch.

236
00:16:22.965 --> 00:16:28.576
The D M S A steps are a person's; in a
test domain, before the pipeline exists,

237
00:16:28.576 --> 00:16:32.191
a person may also send the
pipeline's G M S A step,

238
00:16:32.191 --> 00:16:36.090
which in production the
pipeline's own G M S A sends.

239
00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:41.627
Start the D M S A migration, and complete
it after the thirty-day parallel run.

240
00:16:41.627 --> 00:16:46.390
To back it out, step six runs the undo
command from Microsoft Learn's page on

241
00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:49.981
setting up delegated managed
service accounts, by hand,

242
00:16:49.981 --> 00:16:53.728
because the code never runs it,
re-enables the test account,

243
00:16:53.728 --> 00:16:57.866
removes the test objects by hand,
and deletes the output folders.

244
00:16:57.866 --> 00:17:00.015
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

245
00:17:00.015 --> 00:17:03.520
The workbook's Chapter 6
has the exact commands.

246
00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:08.657
The federal requirement.

247
00:17:08.657 --> 00:17:13.562
The Kerberos accounts are the on-premises
part of Gate 5, and the guide's

248
00:17:13.562 --> 00:17:17.958
validate step names I A five, I A
nine and A U two for the whole gate.

249
00:17:17.958 --> 00:17:22.020
These are NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three controls.

250
00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.485
The module's table gives the
workbook chapter's main rows,

251
00:17:25.485 --> 00:17:27.033
with each step's evidence.

252
00:17:27.033 --> 00:17:30.521
For authenticator management
and change control,

253
00:17:30.521 --> 00:17:33.844
the plan, with the register's
and each input's hash.

254
00:17:33.844 --> 00:17:36.120
For account management, the findings.

255
00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:41.400
For least privilege and access restrictions
for change, the rights each task holds,

256
00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.532
who sends each call, and the reviewed
list of password retrievers.

257
00:17:45.532 --> 00:17:50.360
For automated system account
management and separation of duties,

258
00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:54.498
the refused rows and the run-as
record in each apply folder:

259
00:17:54.498 --> 00:17:58.895
the pipeline sends only its steps,
a person only the D M S A steps.

260
00:17:58.895 --> 00:18:01.647
For account management and change control,

261
00:18:01.647 --> 00:18:05.470
the SailPoint request and
ServiceNow change on every write.

262
00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.120
For the inventory, the island
list and the reconciliation.

263
00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:12.218
And for change control
and disabling accounts,

264
00:18:12.218 --> 00:18:16.745
the results and manifest in each
apply folder, with each write re-read.

265
00:18:16.745 --> 00:18:20.875
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

266
00:18:20.875 --> 00:18:23.503
the agency lines its
evidence up with them,

267
00:18:23.503 --> 00:18:27.920
and which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

268
00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:33.645
Check yourself with two questions.

269
00:18:33.645 --> 00:18:37.620
First: an account is kept
on Kerberos, Keep Kerberos.

270
00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:38.745
What must it have?

271
00:18:38.745 --> 00:18:42.839
The answer is b: the authorizing
official's acceptance,

272
00:18:42.839 --> 00:18:47.933
an S P N owner and an end date no later
than G 5, or the island's end of life.

273
00:18:47.933 --> 00:18:54.392
That's Chapter 3's rule for an account
kept on Kerberos, recorded by D oh eight;

274
00:18:54.392 --> 00:18:57.518
a kept G M S A or D M
S A has the same bound,

275
00:18:57.518 --> 00:19:00.957
and needs an S P N owner
only if it has S P N's.

276
00:19:00.957 --> 00:19:05.795
The check enforces it with N
P I K R B oh five and oh six.

277
00:19:05.795 --> 00:19:10.277
Second: why would this chapter
block a D M S A for svc webfront,

278
00:19:10.277 --> 00:19:13.295
the reason its owner kept it on Kerberos?

279
00:19:13.295 --> 00:19:14.520
The answer is b.

280
00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.983
The account is trusted for
unconstrained delegation,

281
00:19:17.983 --> 00:19:22.678
and Microsoft Learn's delegated
managed service account documentation

282
00:19:22.678 --> 00:19:26.295
says that stops working once
a D M S A migration completes.

283
00:19:26.295 --> 00:19:30.048
That's N P I K R B oh four,
and it's why the owner kept

284
00:19:30.048 --> 00:19:34.195
the account on Kerberos
instead of planning a D M S A.

285
00:19:34.195 --> 00:19:36.622
The module page has three more questions,

286
00:19:36.622 --> 00:19:39.480
each linked to the
section that answers it.

287
00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:44.055
To sum up.

288
00:19:44.055 --> 00:19:47.897
You checked nine Kerberos
accounts in a fictional domain,

289
00:19:47.897 --> 00:19:51.179
read a plan with five writes,
each with its request,

290
00:19:51.179 --> 00:19:56.541
change record and the identity that sends
